From: nitroglow-re VBIOS track
Subject: [PROPOSAL - NOT BUILT, NOT APPLIED] drm/amdgpu: register the board OEM
 i2c line as a bit-banging adapter on atomfirmware ASICs

*** THIS IS A PROPOSAL ONLY. It has not been compiled, applied or loaded. ***

Base: linux v7.1.5 (running kernel 7.1.5-arch1-2). Sources used for the context
lines were fetched read-only into ref/linux-7.1.5/ from
  https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/stable/linux.git/plain/<path>?h=v7.1.5

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RATIONALE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

amdgpu already knows how to expose a board-specific ("OEM") i2c line as a plain
i2c-algo-bit adapter driven over MMIO -- amdgpu_atombios_oem_i2c_init(), which
produces the adapter name

    "AMDGPU i2c bit bus OEM 0x97"

(amdgpu_atombios.c:169 sprintf(stmp, "OEM 0x%x", i2c.i2c_id)
 -> amdgpu_i2c.c:192-193 "AMDGPU i2c bit bus %s")

This is byte-for-byte the string that OpenRGB whitelists at
ref/OpenRGB/i2c_smbus/i2c_amd_gpu.h:23. However that path is unreachable on
Navi 10 for three independent reasons:

 1. amdgpu_device.c:3987-4007 calls amdgpu_i2c_init() only in the `else` arm of
    `if (adev->is_atom_fw)`. Navi 10 has is_atom_fw = true, so amdgpu_i2c_init()
    is never called at all.
 2. amdgpu_i2c.c:220-234 gates the DC branch on
    CHIP_POLARIS10/POLARIS11/POLARIS12 only.
 3. amdgpu_atombios_oem_i2c_init() walks the legacy GPIO_I2C_Info data table,
    which does not exist in an atomfirmware image. Verified on this card's ROM:
    master data table index 10 (file offset 0x92D6) == 0x0000.

On atomfirmware the same information lives in two tables, and this card's VBIOS
(Sapphire RX 5700 XT Nitro+, 1DA2:E409) states it explicitly:

  firmwareinfo (master idx 4) @0xD240, rev 3.3, bytes at +0x30:
      02 97 50 00
      ^  ^  ^
      |  |  +-- board_i2c_feature_slave_addr = 0x50 (8-bit wire format;
      |  |      see amdgpu_ras_eeprom.c:184-194 for the convention on the
      |  |      sibling field) -> 7-bit 0x28
      |  +----- board_i2c_feature_gpio_id    = 0x97
      +-------- board_i2c_feature_id         = 0x02 (== "OEM i2c present",
                the same test DC does at bios_parser2.c:1934)

  gpio_pin_lut (master idx 12) @0xCA82, rev 2.1, entry 12 @0xCAE6:
      a9 5d 00 00 08 08 97 00
      data_a_reg_index = 0x00005DA9, gpio_bitshift = 8,
      gpio_mask_bitshift = 8, gpio_id = 0x97

  0x5DA9 == DCN_BASE__INST0_SEG2 (0x34C0, navi10_ip_offset.h:269)
          + mmDC_GPIO_DDCVGA_A  (0x28E9, dcn_2_0_0_offset.h:12860)

The identification of the pad rests on that register arithmetic alone, not on
any assumption about what the ATOM lane mux field conventionally means: with the
same base, all eight distinct data_a_reg_index values in this ROM's gpio_pin_lut
resolve to named DCN2.0 registers with no misses (0x5D91..0x5DA5 = DDC1_A..DDC6_A,
0x5DA9 = DDCVGA_A, 0x5DB5 = HPD_A). For completeness, GPIO_DDC_LINE_I2C_PAD is
not a possibility on this ASIC: there is no mmDC_GPIO_I2CPAD_* register block in
dcn_2_0_0_offset.h, and hw_translate_dcn20.c:178 records it as
"case REG(DC_GPIO_I2CPAD_A): not exit".

i.e. the OEM line is the DC_GPIO_DDCVGA pin pair. DC does create a ddc_service
for it and amdgpu_dm exposes it as "AMDGPU DM i2c OEM bus", but that path can
only ever use DC's software engine (link == NULL forces hw_supported = false at
link_ddc.c:131-134, and GPIO_DDC_LINE_DDC_VGA == 6 is not < res_cap->num_ddc == 6
at dce_i2c_hw.c:454-458 / dcn20_resource.c:691). That is the only bus on the
test machine that uses dc's software bit-bang engine at all, and a single-address
read there returns -EIO for every address tried. Whether that is an empty bus or
a defect in dc's software engine could not be determined by observation alone;
this patch adds a second, independent access path to the same pads using the
long-standing i2c-algo-bit implementation in amdgpu_i2c.c, which is both a fix
if it is a dc defect and the cheapest experiment that distinguishes the two.

Register derivation used below, all verified against the DCN 2.0 headers:
  * Each DC_GPIO_* pad group is a fixed 4-register block MASK, A, EN, Y:
    mmDC_GPIO_DDCVGA_MASK/_A/_EN/_Y = 0x28E8/0x28E9/0x28EA/0x28EB
    (dcn_2_0_0_offset.h:12858-12864) -> MASK = A-1, EN = A+1, Y = A+2.
  * In every DC_GPIO_DDC* register the clock bit is bit 0 (0x00000001) and the
    data bit is bit 8 (0x00000100), in MASK/A/EN/Y alike
    (dcn_2_0_0_sh_mask.h:49221-49245). dc hardcodes the same two bits at
    hw_translate_dcn20.c:201 and :231. NB the ATOM entry supplies only the DATA
    bit shift; that CLK is bit 0 is taken from the headers and from dc, not from
    the VBIOS.
  * The MASK/A/EN/Y stride is a property of the DC_GPIO_* pad blocks, not of the
    register space as a whole (e.g. mmDC_GPIO_TX12_EN is an EN with no matching
    A/Y). The helper below therefore refuses any LUT entry that is not flagged
    I2C_HW_CAP, and additionally requires the entry to sit in the same
    4-dword-strided block as at least one other I2C_HW_CAP entry, which is what
    makes the derivation safe rather than merely convenient.
  * RREG32(0x5DA9) is a direct MMIO access: 0x5DA9 << 2 == 0x176A4 == 95908,
    inside the 512 KiB register window (dmesg: "register mmio size: 524288").

Behaviour is unchanged for !is_atom_fw ASICs, and for is_atom_fw ASICs whose
VBIOS does not set board_i2c_feature_id == 0x2 the new function returns
immediately.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.h b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.h
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.h
@@ -45,5 +45,6 @@ bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_sram_ecc_supported(struct amdgpu_device *adev);
 bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_ras_rom_addr(struct amdgpu_device *adev, uint8_t *i2c_address);
 bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_mem_training_supported(struct amdgpu_device *adev);
 bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_dynamic_boot_config_supported(struct amdgpu_device *adev);
+void amdgpu_atomfirmware_oem_i2c_init(struct amdgpu_device *adev);
 int amdgpu_atomfirmware_get_fw_reserved_fb_size(struct amdgpu_device *adev);
 int amdgpu_atomfirmware_asic_init(struct amdgpu_device *adev, bool fb_reset);

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_atomfirmware.c
@@ -24,7 +24,8 @@
 #include <drm/amdgpu_drm.h>
 #include "amdgpu.h"
 #include "atomfirmware.h"
 #include "amdgpu_atomfirmware.h"
+#include "amdgpu_i2c.h"
 #include "atom.h"
 #include "atombios.h"
 #include "soc15_hw_ip.h"
@@ -809,6 +810,221 @@ bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_ras_rom_addr(struct amdgpu_device *adev,
 	return false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Return true if gpio_pin[idx] sits in the same 4-dword-strided register block
+ * as at least one other I2C_HW_CAP entry, i.e. it really is one pad of a
+ * DC_GPIO_<pad>_{MASK,A,EN,Y} quad. Cheap structural check, no ASIC tables.
+ */
+static bool amdgpu_atomfirmware_gpio_pin_in_i2c_block(
+		struct atom_gpio_pin_lut_v2_1 *lut, int count, int idx)
+{
+	u32 a_reg = le32_to_cpu(lut->gpio_pin[idx].data_a_reg_index);
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+		u32 other;
+
+		if (i == idx)
+			continue;
+		if (!(lut->gpio_pin[i].gpio_id & I2C_HW_CAP))
+			continue;
+
+		other = le32_to_cpu(lut->gpio_pin[i].data_a_reg_index);
+		if (other == a_reg)
+			continue;
+		if (a_reg > other && (a_reg - other) % 4 == 0)
+			return true;
+		if (other > a_reg && (other - a_reg) % 4 == 0)
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
+/**
+ * amdgpu_atomfirmware_get_bus_rec_for_gpio_pin - build an i2c bus rec
+ * @pin: one atom_gpio_pin_assignment entry from the gpio_pin_lut data table
+ *
+ * atomfirmware images have no GPIO_I2C_Info table, so unlike
+ * amdgpu_atombios_get_bus_rec_for_i2c_gpio() we only get a single register
+ * index and a single bit shift and have to derive the rest.
+ *
+ * @pin->data_a_reg_index is the absolute (SOC15) dword register index of the
+ * DC_GPIO_<pad>_A register, i.e. DCN_BASE__INST0_SEG<n> + mmDC_GPIO_<pad>_A.
+ * Every DC_GPIO_* pad group is a fixed 4-register block in the order
+ * MASK, A, EN, Y -- e.g. on Navi 10 mmDC_GPIO_DDCVGA_MASK/_A/_EN/_Y are
+ * 0x28e8/0x28e9/0x28ea/0x28eb -- so MASK = A - 1, EN = A + 1, Y = A + 2.
+ *
+ * Within each of those registers the clock bit is bit 0 and the data bit is
+ * bit 8; the LUT entry carries the data bit shift. dc makes the same
+ * assumption, see id_to_offset() in
+ * drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/gpio/dcn20/hw_translate_dcn20.c which
+ * hardcodes DC_GPIO_DDC6_A__DC_GPIO_DDC6CLK_A_MASK / ..._DDC6DATA_A_MASK for
+ * every DDC line.
+ */
+static struct amdgpu_i2c_bus_rec
+amdgpu_atomfirmware_get_bus_rec_for_gpio_pin(struct atom_gpio_pin_assignment *pin)
+{
+	struct amdgpu_i2c_bus_rec i2c;
+	u32 a_reg;
+
+	memset(&i2c, 0, sizeof(struct amdgpu_i2c_bus_rec));
+
+	/*
+	 * Only i2c pin pairs have the MASK/A/EN/Y quad; a plain GPIO or HPD
+	 * entry (I2C_HW_CAP clear) shares one register with other pins and the
+	 * derivation below would produce nonsense for it.
+	 */
+	if (!(pin->gpio_id & I2C_HW_CAP))
+		return i2c;
+
+	if (pin->gpio_bitshift > 31 || pin->gpio_mask_bitshift > 31)
+		return i2c;
+
+	a_reg = le32_to_cpu(pin->data_a_reg_index);
+	if (a_reg < 1)
+		return i2c;
+
+	i2c.mask_clk_reg  = a_reg - 1;
+	i2c.mask_data_reg = a_reg - 1;
+	i2c.a_clk_reg     = a_reg;
+	i2c.a_data_reg    = a_reg;
+	i2c.en_clk_reg    = a_reg + 1;
+	i2c.en_data_reg   = a_reg + 1;
+	i2c.y_clk_reg     = a_reg + 2;
+	i2c.y_data_reg    = a_reg + 2;
+
+	i2c.mask_clk_mask = 1 << 0;
+	i2c.a_clk_mask    = 1 << 0;
+	i2c.en_clk_mask   = 1 << 0;
+	i2c.y_clk_mask    = 1 << 0;
+
+	i2c.mask_data_mask = 1 << pin->gpio_mask_bitshift;
+	i2c.a_data_mask    = 1 << pin->gpio_bitshift;
+	i2c.en_data_mask   = 1 << pin->gpio_bitshift;
+	i2c.y_data_mask    = 1 << pin->gpio_bitshift;
+
+	i2c.i2c_id = pin->gpio_id;
+
+	/*
+	 * Always use the bit-banging algo. amdgpu_i2c_create() would otherwise
+	 * pick amdgpu_atombios_i2c_xfer() when amdgpu_hw_i2c is set, and the
+	 * legacy ATOM hw-i2c command tables it drives do not exist in an
+	 * atomfirmware image. Keeping this false also keeps the adapter name
+	 * "AMDGPU i2c bit bus OEM 0x..", which is what userspace expects.
+	 */
+	i2c.hw_capable = false;
+	i2c.mm_i2c = false;
+	i2c.valid = true;
+
+	return i2c;
+}
+
+/**
+ * amdgpu_atomfirmware_oem_i2c_init - expose the board OEM i2c line
+ * @adev: amdgpu_device pointer
+ *
+ * The atomfirmware equivalent of amdgpu_atombios_oem_i2c_init(). Boards that
+ * hang a vendor device (RGB controller, board EEPROM, ...) off a spare i2c pin
+ * pair advertise it in the firmwareinfo table: board_i2c_feature_id == 0x2
+ * means the feature is present, board_i2c_feature_gpio_id names the gpio_id to
+ * look up in gpio_pin_lut, and board_i2c_feature_slave_addr is the device's
+ * 8-bit wire-format address (see the comment in amdgpu_ras_eeprom.c for the
+ * convention used by the sibling ras_rom_i2c_slave_addr field).
+ *
+ * dc consumes the same two fields to build its "AMDGPU DM i2c OEM bus"
+ * (dc/bios/bios_parser2.c get_firmware_info_v3_* and
+ * dc/resource/dcn20/dcn20_resource.c), but that bus is restricted to dc's
+ * software i2c engine. Registering a plain i2c-algo-bit adapter here gives an
+ * independent path to the same pads.
+ */
+void amdgpu_atomfirmware_oem_i2c_init(struct amdgpu_device *adev)
+{
+	struct amdgpu_mode_info *mode_info = &adev->mode_info;
+	struct atom_gpio_pin_lut_v2_1 *pin_lut;
+	union firmware_info *firmware_info;
+	struct amdgpu_i2c_bus_rec i2c;
+	u16 data_offset, size;
+	u8 frev, crev;
+	u8 oem_id, oem_addr;
+	int index, i, num_indices;
+	char stmp[32];
+
+	if (!mode_info->atom_context)
+		return;
+
+	index = get_index_into_master_table(atom_master_list_of_data_tables_v2_1,
+					    firmwareinfo);
+	if (!amdgpu_atom_parse_data_header(mode_info->atom_context, index,
+					   &size, &frev, &crev, &data_offset))
+		return;
+	if (frev != 3)
+		return;
+
+	firmware_info = (union firmware_info *)
+		(mode_info->atom_context->bios + data_offset);
+
+	/*
+	 * board_i2c_feature_id / _gpio_id / _slave_addr sit at the same
+	 * offsets (+0x30 / +0x31 / +0x32) in atom_firmware_info_v3_1 through
+	 * v3_5, so any of the union members will do; dc reads them through the
+	 * v3_2 layout for the same reason.
+	 */
+	if (firmware_info->v32.board_i2c_feature_id != 0x2)
+		return;
+
+	oem_id   = firmware_info->v32.board_i2c_feature_gpio_id;
+	oem_addr = firmware_info->v32.board_i2c_feature_slave_addr;
+
+	index = get_index_into_master_table(atom_master_list_of_data_tables_v2_1,
+					    gpio_pin_lut);
+	if (!amdgpu_atom_parse_data_header(mode_info->atom_context, index,
+					   &size, &frev, &crev, &data_offset))
+		return;
+	if (size < sizeof(struct atom_common_table_header) +
+		   sizeof(struct atom_gpio_pin_assignment))
+		return;
+
+	pin_lut = (struct atom_gpio_pin_lut_v2_1 *)
+		(mode_info->atom_context->bios + data_offset);
+
+	num_indices = (size - sizeof(struct atom_common_table_header)) /
+		sizeof(struct atom_gpio_pin_assignment);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < num_indices; i++) {
+		if (pin_lut->gpio_pin[i].gpio_id != oem_id)
+			continue;
+
+		/*
+		 * Sanity: the pad must live in the same 4-dword-strided block
+		 * as the other i2c pin pairs. This is what lets us derive
+		 * MASK/EN/Y from the A register index instead of hardcoding a
+		 * per-ASIC table the way dc does.
+		 */
+		if (!amdgpu_atomfirmware_gpio_pin_in_i2c_block(pin_lut,
+							       num_indices, i))
+			break;
+
+		i2c = amdgpu_atomfirmware_get_bus_rec_for_gpio_pin(
+				&pin_lut->gpio_pin[i]);
+		if (!i2c.valid)
+			break;
+
+		sprintf(stmp, "OEM 0x%x", i2c.i2c_id);
+		adev->i2c_bus[0] = amdgpu_i2c_create(adev_to_drm(adev), &i2c, stmp);
+		if (adev->i2c_bus[0])
+			dev_info(adev->dev,
+				 "OEM i2c line 0x%02x: DC_GPIO A reg 0x%05x, slave 0x%02x (7-bit 0x%02x)\n",
+				 i2c.i2c_id, i2c.a_data_reg,
+				 oem_addr, oem_addr >> 1);
+		else
+			dev_warn(adev->dev,
+				 "failed to register OEM i2c line 0x%02x\n",
+				 oem_id);
+		break;
+	}
+}
+
 
 union smu_info {
 	struct atom_smu_info_v3_1 v31;
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_i2c.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_i2c.c
